Question: Determine whether the following two code snippets are semantic code clones.

Code 1: Python
```python
n = int(input())
total = 0
for i in range(1, n + 1):
    if i % 3 == 0 or i % 5 == 0:
        total += i
print(total)
```

Code 2: Java
```java
import java.util.*;
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Scanner sc = new Scanner(System.in);
        int n = sc.nextInt();
        int total = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            if (i % 3 == 0 || i % 5 == 0) {
                total += i;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(total);
    }
}
```
<analysis>
Code 1 reads a single integer n from standard input, sums every value in 1..n that is divisible by 3 or by 5, and prints the total.<end_of_step>

Code 2 reads a single integer n from standard input with a Scanner, sums every value in 1..n divisible by 3 or by 5, and prints the total. Both sides are complete stdin-to-stdout programs.<end_of_step>

They look equivalent, but the loop bounds and the divisibility condition are exactly the kind of detail that is easy to misread. Rather than predict Code 2's output, run both programs on the same inputs and compare what they actually print.<end_of_step>
<end_of_analysis>

